and then cool shit magically
appears in his room.


00:57:01.885 --> 00:57:04.421
He doesn't realize that
this is actually a job


00:57:04.521 --> 00:57:06.256
and there's sacrifice
that comes along with it.


00:57:06.356 --> 00:57:09.259
So I told him. I said, "Listen,
Frankie, how 'bout this?


00:57:09.359 --> 00:57:14.198
"How 'bout you explain to me why
I should get you a new phone,


00:57:14.298 --> 00:57:17.741
"and if it makes sense,
I'll do it right now. Go."


00:57:18.035 --> 00:57:19.737
"I need one."


00:57:20.604 --> 00:57:23.874
I said, "No, Frankie. You're a teenager.
You don't need a phone.


00:57:23.974 --> 00:57:26.710
"A cell phone for a
teenager isn't a necessity.


00:57:26.810 --> 00:57:29.179
"It's a luxury, okay?


00:57:29.279 --> 00:57:33.325
"It's not a necessity,
it's a luxury."


00:57:39.923 --> 00:57:42.793
He says, "What if
there's an emergency?"


00:57:42.893 --> 00:57:45.796
I go, "Like what?
Someone taking your phone?


00:57:45.896 --> 00:57:47.664
"What did you do today when
they took your phone?"


00:57:47.764 --> 00:57:51.001
"I went to the...
I went to the cafeteria,


00:57:51.101 --> 00:57:52.302
"and I talked to my friend Angel


00:57:52.402 --> 00:57:54.338
"and he let me use his phone,
and I called my mom."


00:57:54.438 --> 00:57:55.839
And I go, "And guess what?
If that doesn't work,


00:57:55.939 --> 00:57:57.474
"you can go to the office
and let them know,


00:57:57.574 --> 00:58:01.347
"'There's a family emergency,
please contact my mom or my dad.' Next."


00:58:01.578 --> 00:58:03.180
"What if you need to
get a hold of me?"


00:58:03.280 --> 00:58:05.824
"I know what school you go to."


00:58:06.116 --> 00:58:07.751
"What about when I'm walking home?"


00:58:07.851 --> 00:58:09.887
"I know the route that you take."


00:58:09.987 --> 00:58:12.823
And then he pulled this one out,
one of the greatest lines ever.


00:58:12.923 --> 00:58:16.570
"You know what?
You talk a lot about me on TV.


00:58:16.860 --> 00:58:19.131
"People know who I am now.


00:58:21.264 --> 00:58:23.637
"What if someone tries to kidnap me?"


00:58:26.303 --> 00:58:29.143
I said, "You weigh 225. Good luck.


00:58:31.641 --> 00:58:33.110
"They are not kidnapping your ass


00:58:33.210 --> 00:58:34.878
"without an iPhone
and a box of Oreos.


00:58:34.978 --> 00:58:38.048
"That's the only way
that's going to happen."


00:58:38.148 --> 00:58:40.250
I said, "Frankie, do you
realize how lucky you are?


00:58:40.350 --> 00:58:43.053
"You had a phone. I didn't
have a phone in school.


00:58:43.153 --> 00:58:44.755
"Your mom didn't have
a phone in school.


00:58:44.855 --> 00:58:46.223
"If we needed to make a phone call,


00:58:46.323 --> 00:58:49.626
"we had to carry change,
or we had to call collect."


00:58:49.726 --> 00:58:51.997
"What's that?" Oh, my God!


00:58:52.796 --> 00:58:56.033
Trying to explain the concept
of a collect call to my kid


00:58:56.133 --> 00:58:58.201
is like trying to
explain rocket science.


00:58:58.301 --> 00:58:59.870
He'll never have to deal with it.


00:58:59.970 --> 00:59:01.805
He'll never know what it's
like to be out late somewhere


00:59:01.905 --> 00:59:03.106
and have to use a phone.


00:59:03.206 --> 00:59:06.209
First of all, to find a pay phone.


00:59:06.309 --> 00:59:08.445
If you see a pay phone now,
you look at it like,


00:59:08.545 --> 00:59:10.714
"Oh, wow.
They forgot to take it down.


00:59:10.814 --> 00:59:12.816
"Get your camera. Take a picture.


00:59:12.916 --> 00:59:15.786
"Cause that shit's gone.
Take a picture."


00:59:15.886 --> 00:59:17.721
He'll never know about
staying out late at night


00:59:17.821 --> 00:59:19.589
and having to use one
of those nasty phones


00:59:19.689 --> 00:59:20.924
and taking off that receiver.


00:59:21.024 --> 00:59:22.559
You know, the nasty one that has gum


00:59:22.659 --> 00:59:24.161
and gonorrhea all over it, right?


00:59:24.261 --> 00:59:25.762
And you got to keep it
far away from your face,


00:59:25.862 --> 00:59:27.264
so you don't get infected.


00:59:27.364 --> 00:59:29.566
And then you make that phone call,
you know, that freaking...


00:59:29.666 --> 00:59:31.668
When you make a collect
call back in the day?


00:59:31.768 --> 00:59:34.404
It was expensive. He's not
going to have to do that now.


00:59:34.504 --> 00:59:37.441
Back in the day, if you made a
collect call, it was a mission.


00:59:37.541 --> 00:59:39.509
It was a mission,
and it was expensive.


00:59:39.609 --> 00:59:41.278
If you called my mom collect,


00:59:41.378 --> 00:59:43.148
it better have been life or death


00:59:43.680 --> 00:59:45.723
or that's what it was going to be.


00:59:46.249 --> 00:59:47.951
You call my mom back
in the day, shoot,


00:59:48.051 --> 00:59:50.120
we were on welfare and Section 8.


00:59:50.220 --> 00:59:51.955
Every penny counted.


00:59:52.055 --> 00:59:53.256
You call her up.


00:59:54.724 --> 00:59:55.826
"Hello."


00:59:55.926 --> 00:59:57.527
And then she'd get the presentation.


00:59:57.627 --> 00:59:59.170
"AT&T.


00:59:59.629 --> 01:00:01.932
"Do you accept a collect call from...


01:00:02.032 --> 01:00:05.268
"Caller, at the tone,
say only your name."


01:00:05.368 --> 01:00:07.604
You had to get creative.


01:00:07.704 --> 01:00:08.772
"Mom, it's me. Pick me
up at 7-Eleven at 6:00.


01:00:08.872 --> 01:00:10.941
"Hurry up. I love you. Bye."


01:00:11.041 --> 01:00:12.642
"Do you accept the charges?"


01:00:12.742 --> 01:00:14.785
"Hell, no. I'm on my way, mijo."


01:00:27.390 --> 01:00:30.293
And if you couldn't get
the whole message out,


01:00:30.393 --> 01:00:33.463
you had to call back, do that
whole process all over again,


01:00:33.563 --> 01:00:36.400
and then say another sentence
until they cut you off.


01:00:36.500 --> 01:00:39.112
I told Frankie we call
that "ghetto texting."


01:00:40.437 --> 01:00:42.906
'Cause he'll never have to do it.


01:00:43.006 --> 01:00:46.243
The problem is, is that
my son is very spoiled,


01:00:46.343 --> 01:00:49.679
and I realize that it's my fault.
It's 100% my fault.


01:00:49.779 --> 01:00:50.914
When he first came into my life,


01:00:51.014 --> 01:00:53.683
when he was seven years old,
he was very appreciative.


01:00:53.783 --> 01:00:55.919
Everything was, "Thank you."
Everything was like, "Oh, my God.


01:00:56.019 --> 01:00:57.487
"This is so cool. Thank you.
Thank you. Thank you."


01:00:57.587 --> 01:01:00.961
Big old hug. "Thank you.
I love you. Thank you."


01:01:02.759 --> 01:01:05.662
And what I did was, is that
I started giving him stuff,


01:01:05.762 --> 01:01:07.330
and I never expected anything for it,


01:01:07.430 --> 01:01:09.666
and, you know, with me,
I was always like, you know,


01:01:09.766 --> 01:01:12.602
I work really hard
to have nice things,


01:01:12.702 --> 01:01:15.005
and is it a crime that I want
my family to have nice things?


01:01:15.105 --> 01:01:16.606
I figure, you know what?
I went through a lot.


01:01:16.706 --> 01:01:18.542
I just want my son to have the best.


01:01:18.642 --> 01:01:20.277
But you can't always
give 'em everything,


01:01:20.377 --> 01:01:23.346
because then they don't learn
to appreciate and value things,


01:01:23.446 --> 01:01:26.149
and I had to find out the hard way.


01:01:33.056 --> 01:01:35.025
It takes a lot for me to tell
you guys that, by the way,


01:01:35.125 --> 01:01:37.094
that I freaking messed up.
My girl pointed it out.


01:01:37.194 --> 01:01:39.129
She goes, "You're messing up."


01:01:39.229 --> 01:01:40.864
I go, "What am I doing wrong?"


01:01:40.964 --> 01:01:44.034
She goes, "You don't let him earn
anything. You don't let him try.


01:01:44.134 --> 01:01:45.802
"You don't let him make an attempt


01:01:45.902 --> 01:01:47.104
"to try to work toward something,


01:01:47.204 --> 01:01:49.106
"so of course he doesn't
appreciate things."


01:01:49.206 --> 01:01:53.354
And it was very evident last month,
you know, birthday time?


01:01:54.477 --> 01:01:57.047
I bought him a pair of Jordans.


01:01:57.147 --> 01:01:58.915
Not just a regular pair of Jordans.


01:01:59.015 --> 01:02:02.219
They were a special collector's
edition pair of Jordans.


01:02:02.319 --> 01:02:05.589
You know, the kind of Jordans where
people wait in line the night before


01:02:05.689 --> 01:02:06.756
just so they can buy these shoes.


01:02:06.856 --> 01:02:09.659
Except I didn't,
'cause I got the hookup!


01:02:09.759 --> 01:02:11.370
Yeah.


01:02:12.662 --> 01:02:16.099
They were really nice shoes,
$180 pair of shoes, and...


01:02:16.199 --> 01:02:18.401
Yeah. Hey, lot of jokes. So...


01:02:19.436 --> 01:02:20.570
I didn't even wrap 'em, okay?


01:02:20.670 --> 01:02:23.740
I put 'em on the box, and I waited
for him to walk in the room.


01:02:23.840 --> 01:02:25.342
And I'm standing there,
and he walks in.


01:02:25.442 --> 01:02:28.445
I go, "Happy birthday,"
and he sees the shoes.


01:02:28.545 --> 01:02:31.481
He looks at the shoes,
and all he says is,


01:02:31.581 --> 01:02:34.251
"Cool." That's it. "Cool."


01:02:34.351 --> 01:02:36.019
Sometimes I'll get lucky,
he'll say it twice.


01:02:36.119 --> 01:02:37.730
"Cool, cool."


01:02:38.355 --> 01:02:40.657
And then he walks around to see
what other presents there are,


01:02:40.757 --> 01:02:43.260
and I told his mom,
"Did you see that? Did you see..."


01:02:43.360 --> 01:02:46.463
"Don't get mad. I told you,
you don't let him earn anything.


01:02:46.563 --> 01:02:49.573
"Now that's why he acts that way.
He expects things."


01:02:50.100 --> 01:02:51.608
I'm like, "Oh."


01:02:52.135 --> 01:02:54.337
And I want to get upset,
but she's absolutely right.


01:02:54.437 --> 01:02:56.439
He's at the point now of spoiled


01:02:56.539 --> 01:02:58.108
where he walks up to me, and he goes,


01:02:58.208 --> 01:03:00.810
"Dad, I'm really bored
with my Nintendo Wii.


01:03:00.910 --> 01:03:02.445
"Can I give it to my friend Angel?"


01:03:02.545 --> 01:03:04.681
I go, "What did you just say?"


01:03:04.781 --> 01:03:07.184
"I'm bored with my Nintendo Wii.
Can I give it away?"


01:03:07.284 --> 01:03:08.451
I go, "Is it broken?"


01:03:08.551 --> 01:03:09.986
"No."


01:03:10.086 --> 01:03:12.956
"How long have you had it?"
"Ooh, like, four years."


01:03:13.056 --> 01:03:15.358
I go, "How many games do you
have for the Nintendo Wii?"


01:03:15.458 --> 01:03:16.726
"Like, 300."


01:03:17.927 --> 01:03:19.868
I could feel you
judging me over there.


01:03:20.897 --> 01:03:22.508
I felt that. "Oh!"


01:03:23.233 --> 01:03:24.734
You're freaking...
You're judging me right now.


01:03:24.834 --> 01:03:26.836
I can feel it.
She's over there, she's like,


01:03:26.936 --> 01:03:29.539
"This is some Dr. Phil
shit right here.


01:03:29.639 --> 01:03:30.840
"He really messed him up."


01:03:30.940 --> 01:03:32.141
Yeah, I heard you.


01:03:32.742 --> 01:03:35.145
Let me just for the record,
let me set this straight, okay?


01:03:35.245 --> 01:03:38.682
I did not buy my son 300 games.


01:03:38.782 --> 01:03:40.083
Here's what happened.


01:03:40.183 --> 01:03:43.320
I have a friend who's
a computer hacker,


01:03:43.420 --> 01:03:45.822
and for 75 bucks,


01:03:45.922 --> 01:03:48.864
he put 300 games on
my son's hard drive.


01:03:49.359 --> 01:03:52.262
Yes, I have money,
but I'm still ghetto.


01:03:57.434 --> 01:03:58.669
Oh, yeah.


01:03:59.536 --> 01:04:01.905
So I tell Frankie, "Do you
realize how lucky you are?"


01:04:02.005 --> 01:04:04.007
And then he rolls his eyes.
"Oh, lucky."


01:04:04.107 --> 01:04:05.408
I go, "Yeah, dude, you are."


01:04:05.508 --> 01:04:07.577
I says, "You got a
Nintendo Wii that works.


01:04:07.677 --> 01:04:09.679
"You've had it for four years,
and it still works.


01:04:09.779 --> 01:04:12.015
"If something happens to it,
loan take it back to the store,


01:04:12.115 --> 01:04:14.017
"get you a new one,
because I got a warranty


01:04:14.117 --> 01:04:15.919
"that'll last you
another four years."


01:04:16.019 --> 01:04:17.387
"Why is that a big deal?"


01:04:17.487 --> 01:04:19.556
"Because when I was your age,
I had a Nintendo."


01:04:19.656 --> 01:04:21.392
"Wii?" "No.


01:04:22.559 --> 01:04:24.127
"Frankie, in 1987,


01:04:24.227 --> 01:04:28.265
"I had a thing called a Nintendo
Entertainment System, okay?"


01:04:31.401 --> 01:04:34.673
Nintendo Entertainment System.


01:04:35.038 --> 01:04:36.873
It didn't last four years.


01:04:36.973 --> 01:04:38.575
It lasts 90 days.


01:04:38.675 --> 01:04:40.910
90 days is what it took
for you to hit "Power,"


01:04:41.010 --> 01:04:42.946
and start seeing a
flashing red screen.


01:04:43.046 --> 01:04:44.381
You know the flashing red screen,


01:04:44.481 --> 01:04:46.049
where you have to look at
the ground or look away,


01:04:46.149 --> 01:04:49.523
or you have a seizure right
then and there? Oh, yeah.


01:04:49.819 --> 01:04:51.821
And then what you had to do is
you had to flip the Nintendo over,


01:04:51.921 --> 01:04:53.056
and there was a silver sticker,


01:04:53.156 --> 01:04:55.392
a silver sticker with
an 800 number on it,


01:04:55.492 --> 01:04:56.926
and you call the 800 number,


01:04:57.026 --> 01:04:59.062
and they put you in contact
with someone in Japan,


01:04:59.162 --> 01:05:00.964
who made you feel like a pendejo.


01:05:01.064 --> 01:05:02.732
Oh, yeah. He made you feel stupid.


01:05:02.832 --> 01:05:03.967
You call him up, and he's like,


01:05:04.067 --> 01:05:06.936
"It cost 250 to repair Nintendo."


01:05:07.036 --> 01:05:08.471
250 to fix it?


01:05:08.571 --> 01:05:10.774
But it's 150 for a brand-new one.


01:05:10.874 --> 01:05:13.576
And if you couldn't afford
150 for a brand-new one,


01:05:13.676 --> 01:05:16.313
like we couldn't afford
150 for a brand-new one,


01:05:16.413 --> 01:05:17.914
you had to become a technician.


01:05:18.014 --> 01:05:20.417
At the age of seven,
eight, nine, 10, 11,


01:05:20.517 --> 01:05:21.818
you had to go in the kitchen


01:05:21.918 --> 01:05:24.654
and find the most messed-up
butter knife you could.


01:05:24.754 --> 01:05:26.756
Right? The one that had
bend marks and rust stains,


01:05:26.856 --> 01:05:28.858
and take that sucker
back in the living room,


01:05:28.958 --> 01:05:31.661
because you were going
to perform an operation.


01:05:31.761 --> 01:05:33.530
You were going to perform an operation


01:05:33.630 --> 01:05:36.470
and bring that Nintendo back to life.


01:05:37.534 --> 01:05:38.838
Yeah.


01:05:42.038 --> 01:05:43.807
You had to work to play.


01:05:43.907 --> 01:05:45.375
You had to unplug it,
plug it back in,


01:05:45.475 --> 01:05:46.943
power, reset, power,
reset, power, reset.


01:05:47.043 --> 01:05:50.046
It was like performing CPR.


01:05:50.146 --> 01:05:51.548
You'd hold a cartridge


01:05:51.648 --> 01:05:52.982
and it was like you push
it down, you push it up,


01:05:53.082 --> 01:05:54.984
you push it down, you push it up,
push it down, you push it up,


01:05:55.084 --> 01:05:57.721
push it down, you push it up.
Give it oxygen!


01:06:06.796 --> 01:06:09.032
And if you were lucky,
if you were lucky,


01:06:09.132 --> 01:06:10.900
you'd hear the magical sound.


01:06:12.235 --> 01:06:15.882
"It's alive! It's alive!"


01:06:19.909 --> 01:06:22.812
You were more happy about
making your game work


01:06:22.912 --> 01:06:25.148
than actually playing
it back in the day.


01:06:25.248 --> 01:06:27.317
Frankie will never know about having


01:06:27.417 --> 01:06:30.653
to repair his game system
in order to play it.


01:06:30.753 --> 01:06:32.756
He'll never have to deal
with the cartridge.


01:06:32.856 --> 01:06:35.158
He barely has to touch the disk.


01:06:35.258 --> 01:06:38.228
Most of the games now,
you stream it through Wi-Fi.


01:06:38.328 --> 01:06:39.829
And if the Wi-Fi's messing up,


01:06:39.929 --> 01:06:41.698
you're not going to fix
Wi-Fi the same way


01:06:41.798 --> 01:06:43.833
you'd fix an old-school
Nintendo cartridge.


01:06:43.933 --> 01:06:45.168
That doesn't work.


01:06:45.268 --> 01:06:46.369
"Wi-Fi's out." "Hold on.


01:06:47.604 --> 01:06:48.908
"Try it again."


01:06:49.873 --> 01:06:51.984
You can't touch Wi-Fi.


01:06:52.108 --> 01:06:56.346
Gadgets and devices now are
so delicate and sensitive.


01:06:56.446 --> 01:06:57.447
You can't touch anything.


01:06:57.547 --> 01:06:58.948
Everything requires a specialist


01:06:59.048 --> 01:07:01.518
or a technician to
come over and fix it.


01:07:01.618 --> 01:07:05.288
Back in the day,
gadgets and appliances


01:07:05.388 --> 01:07:08.191
worked so much better
when you applied


01:07:08.291 --> 01:07:10.596
just a little violence to them.


01:07:11.995 --> 01:07:14.130
You didn't have to
call a repairperson.


01:07:14.230 --> 01:07:16.299
If you had a big-screen TV


01:07:16.399 --> 01:07:18.234
and there was lines
going through the middle,


01:07:18.334 --> 01:07:19.706
how would you fix it?


01:07:20.470 --> 01:07:22.705
Right? And what happened?


01:07:22.805 --> 01:07:24.541
Ta-da!


01:07:29.178 --> 01:07:31.648
Let you try that method tonight


01:07:31.748 --> 01:07:33.723
on a flat-screen,
and see what happens.


01:07:34.150 --> 01:07:36.626
"Stupid flat-screen. Shit! Broke it!


01:07:37.487 --> 01:07:40.657
"Pick it up right there.
I got a warranty. Come on. Let's go."


01:07:40.757 --> 01:07:42.659
I tell Frankie, "You are so lucky.


01:07:42.759 --> 01:07:45.395
"You're in an age of
technology that's amazing.


01:07:45.495 --> 01:07:46.765
"You don't have to worry."


01:07:46.930 --> 01:07:48.932
I want him to get it.
I want him to understand.


01:07:49.032 --> 01:07:50.467
I want him to appreciate,


01:07:50.567 --> 01:07:53.570
but at the same time, I got to
learn to freaking check myself.


01:07:53.670 --> 01:07:55.138
Sometimes he says things.


01:07:55.238 --> 01:07:56.473
Sometimes he says things to me,


01:07:56.573 --> 01:08:00.118
and sometimes they sound
a little rude or hurtful.


01:08:00.443 --> 01:08:03.246
For example, every Monday,
when I go home,


01:08:03.346 --> 01:08:06.649
every Monday night, I go home,
and I always take my family out.


01:08:06.749 --> 01:08:08.952
It's always movie night.
I love movie night on Mondays.


01:08:09.052 --> 01:08:12.422
There's no lines. You go in,
you go out, good parking. It's sweet.


01:08:12.522 --> 01:08:15.992
And I always tell Frankie before
we go out, "Hey, one hour.


01:08:16.092 --> 01:08:17.827
"We're leaving, okay, one hour.


01:08:17.927 --> 01:08:19.262
"Make sure you're ready, okay?"


01:08:19.362 --> 01:08:20.630
"Okay." "Good."


01:08:20.730 --> 01:08:23.600
And then I go in the room,
and I take a nap,


01:08:23.700 --> 01:08:25.368
and sometimes I oversleep,


01:08:25.468 --> 01:08:28.271
and so, an hour and a half later,
Frankie comes over to the room.


01:08:28.371 --> 01:08:31.045
He pounds on the door. Door...


01:08:31.975 --> 01:08:34.777
And I wake up. I'm like,
"Hey, what's up, Frankie?"


01:08:34.877 --> 01:08:37.489
"I thought you said we
were leaving in an hour.


01:08:41.117 --> 01:08:43.092
"What happened to an hour?"


01:08:44.587 --> 01:08:46.556
I don't get mad. I don't yell.


01:08:46.656 --> 01:08:48.191
I just look at him, and I say,


01:08:48.291 --> 01:08:51.294
"Please forgive me, sir.
I do apologize.


01:08:51.394 --> 01:08:53.463
"As soon as I am done
washing the horses,


01:08:53.563 --> 01:08:56.676
"I shall take the carriage
to your quarters, sir."


01:08:59.802 --> 01:09:01.606
"Why are you talking
to me like that?"


01:09:01.738 --> 01:09:03.139
"I am speaking to you this way,


01:09:03.239 --> 01:09:06.352
"because you're speaking to
me like I'm an asshole, sir,


01:09:06.743 --> 01:09:08.645
"and I shall not
tolerate such behavior


01:09:08.745 --> 01:09:12.017
"coming from my offspring
that is technically not mine.


01:09:14.584 --> 01:09:16.195
"Do you have anything else, sir?"


01:09:17.020 --> 01:09:19.222
"Can my friends go with
us to the movies?"


01:09:19.322 --> 01:09:21.468
"Oh, the vultures are here.


01:09:22.091 --> 01:09:24.494
"Oh, they are here to pick
at the fruit of my labor.


01:09:24.594 --> 01:09:27.730
"Absolutely, Frankie.
Is there anything else?"


01:09:27.830 --> 01:09:28.831
"What are you going to do?"


01:09:28.931 --> 01:09:30.266
"I am going in the room, sir."


01:09:30.366 --> 01:09:31.334
"To do what?"


01:09:31.434 --> 01:09:33.545
"I'm going to defile
your mother, Frankie.


01:09:34.370 --> 01:09:38.347
"She's been quite chatty,
and someone must put her in her place."


01:09:40.109 --> 01:09:43.580
I stumbled on gold that night.


01:09:43.680 --> 01:09:45.515
I stumbled on gold, you guys.


01:09:45.615 --> 01:09:46.883
When I said that about his mom,


01:09:46.983 --> 01:09:48.518
I found out


01:09:48.618 --> 01:09:51.560
that Frankie is very
sensitive about his mom.


01:09:52.055 --> 01:09:55.858
And he's at the age right now where
affection really bothers him.


01:09:55.958 --> 01:09:59.295
Not necessarily from me, because
if I try to hug him, he's cool with it,


01:09:59.395 --> 01:10:01.030
'cause I give those quick
bro hugs, you know, like,


01:10:01.130 --> 01:10:03.366
"Hey. Hey, what's up?" You know?


01:10:03.466 --> 01:10:06.408
However, if his mom is
standing right next to me


01:10:06.536 --> 01:10:08.771
and I reach over and I grab her hand,


01:10:08.871 --> 01:10:11.874
he sees that as gross,
and he'll make the face.


01:10:11.974 --> 01:10:12.942
"Ugh!"


01:10:13.042 --> 01:10:14.380
"What?"


01:10:14.544 --> 01:10:15.882
"Ah."


01:10:17.013 --> 01:10:18.214
"The hand?"


01:10:18.314 --> 01:10:19.582
"That's gross, Dad."


01:10:19.682 --> 01:10:21.751
I'm like, "Oh, that's not gross,
Frankie. That's not gross,


01:10:21.851 --> 01:10:24.031
"Baby, open your mouth."


01:10:29.992 --> 01:10:33.663
That's his weak spot, and I
go for it every single time.


01:10:33.763 --> 01:10:35.398
One day, I was ironing
one of my shirts


01:10:35.498 --> 01:10:36.466
that I was going to put on, right?


01:10:36.566 --> 01:10:38.801
I'm ironing the shirt, and I'm
talking to him about his grades.


01:10:38.901 --> 01:10:40.503
As I'm talking to him
about his grades,


01:10:40.603 --> 01:10:42.238
there's a car driving
in front of the house,


01:10:42.338 --> 01:10:43.473
and the stereo was really loud,


01:10:43.573 --> 01:10:45.241
and you could feel the bass.


01:10:47.376 --> 01:10:49.988
So I started dancing. "Oh, yeah."


01:10:50.847 --> 01:10:52.315
Frankie starts laughing, right?


01:10:52.415 --> 01:10:55.018
And that felt good.
I was making my son laugh.


01:10:55.118 --> 01:10:57.824
But then he starts pointing.
He starts pointing.


01:10:59.255 --> 01:11:00.490
"Why are you pointing?"


01:11:00.590 --> 01:11:02.959
"'Cause you're fat,
your fat's going everywhere.


01:11:03.059 --> 01:11:04.794
"Your fat's going everywhere."


01:11:04.894 --> 01:11:07.830
I go, "Really? My fat's going
everywhere? Well, guess what?


01:11:07.930 --> 01:11:11.009
"This fat makes your mom horny."


01:11:23.813 --> 01:11:24.958
And he's like...


01:11:28.451 --> 01:11:32.098
If I ask him to do something,
and he doesn't do it,


01:11:32.555 --> 01:11:34.557
like, for example, as parents,


01:11:34.657 --> 01:11:37.326
you want your kids to go
to sleep by a certain time,


01:11:37.426 --> 01:11:40.463
because you need 'em to wake up
in time to get ready for school.


01:11:40.563 --> 01:11:42.865
Frankie's cutoff is 11:00.


01:11:42.965 --> 01:11:45.668
It's pretty late,
if you ask me, for a kid.


01:11:45.768 --> 01:11:48.237
Sometimes he'll push it to midnight,
you know, play dumb.


01:11:48.337 --> 01:11:49.472
"Oh, I didn't know what time it was."


01:11:49.572 --> 01:11:52.275
"Yeah, whatever.
You're only holding a clock."


01:11:53.409 --> 01:11:55.678
But I get it. I used
to play dumb, too.


01:11:55.778 --> 01:11:58.147
So then he'll walk in his room,
and he'll say...


01:11:58.247 --> 01:12:00.516
"Good night, Dad,"
and he'll close the door.


01:12:01.751 --> 01:12:03.186
And he thinks he's slick.


01:12:03.286 --> 01:12:06.255
He takes a towel, and he
starts rolling up the towel,


01:12:06.355 --> 01:12:08.157
and he puts it under the door,


01:12:08.257 --> 01:12:11.301
so that I can't see the
light coming from the TV.


01:12:11.828 --> 01:12:13.162
Sounds pretty genius, right?


01:12:13.262 --> 01:12:16.466
But, hello, cover the
rest of the stupid door.


01:12:17.600 --> 01:12:19.569
He freaking turns on his big-ass TV,


01:12:19.669 --> 01:12:22.645
and it looks like Immigration's
breaking in to his bedroom.


01:12:24.807 --> 01:12:26.509
And so what happens is he'll stay up


01:12:26.609 --> 01:12:28.511
until 2:00, 3:00,
4:00 in the morning,


01:12:28.611 --> 01:12:31.180
and he, you know,
he's supposed to wake up at 6:00.


01:12:31.280 --> 01:12:34.183
His alarm goes off, and he
sleeps through the alarm,


01:12:34.283 --> 01:12:36.018
and it's a real alarm-alarm.


01:12:36.118 --> 01:12:38.054
It's not a radio. It's freaking...


01:12:40.323 --> 01:12:42.161
Whole house awake. Him?


01:12:44.293 --> 01:12:45.695
And it's right here.


01:12:45.795 --> 01:12:47.864
So I got to get out of bed,
and I go over to his room,


01:12:47.964 --> 01:12:51.033
and I open the door, and I kick
the stupid towel out of the way,


01:12:51.133 --> 01:12:52.969
and then I walk over, you know.


01:12:58.140 --> 01:13:00.718
And I yell, "Frankie! Frankie!"


01:13:01.143 --> 01:13:02.712
Nothing, so I shake him.


01:13:02.812 --> 01:13:04.380
"Frankie, wake up."


01:13:04.480 --> 01:13:07.116
You know what happens when
I shake my son in the morning?


01:13:07.216 --> 01:13:09.692
It makes him eat in his sleep.


01:13:10.186 --> 01:13:13.196
That's what happens.
I shake him, and he does this.


01:13:23.366 --> 01:13:24.467
So you know what I do?


01:13:24.567 --> 01:13:28.237
I get on top of the bed,
and I slowly lay on top of him,


01:13:28.337 --> 01:13:31.507
and I apply my full weight,
bro, all of it,


01:13:31.607 --> 01:13:33.787
slowly, and you could hear him.


01:13:37.380 --> 01:13:42.893
"Oh, God! Oh, God!
You're too big! You're too big!"


01:13:43.252 --> 01:13:44.821
And then I whisper in his ear,


01:13:44.921 --> 01:13:47.226
"That's what your mom said."


01:14:00.937 --> 01:14:04.575
I know one day, my son is going to write
a book called Fluffy's Full of Shit.


01:14:06.375 --> 01:14:08.316
It's kind of crazy.


01:14:09.378 --> 01:14:11.380
I talked to his mom.


01:14:11.480 --> 01:14:14.617
I talked to Frankie's mom,
and she tells me, "Listen."


01:14:14.717 --> 01:14:17.620
She goes, "I'm going to tell
you something. Don't get mad.


01:14:17.720 --> 01:14:21.129
"The reason why you and Frankie
are always butting heads


01:14:22.224 --> 01:14:24.768
"is because you guys are
basically the same person."


01:14:25.394 --> 01:14:26.963
And I go, "We are not
the same person."


01:14:27.063 --> 01:14:28.664
She goes,
"Your mannerisms are the same.


01:14:28.764 --> 01:14:29.966
"The way you guys think is the same.


01:14:30.066 --> 01:14:32.668
"You're exactly...
You're moody in the same way.


01:14:32.768 --> 01:14:33.803
"It's the same."


01:14:33.903 --> 01:14:36.005
And keep in mind,
Frankie's technically my stepson,


01:14:36.105 --> 01:14:37.807
so my girl insisting this,
that we're the same,


01:14:37.907 --> 01:14:40.644
I'm like, "Hello, I wasn't
there at the beginning."


01:14:42.511 --> 01:14:44.981
But, no, she insists.
"You guys are the same."


01:14:45.081 --> 01:14:46.649
And I started analyzing our lives.


01:14:46.749 --> 01:14:48.251
I started analyzing Frankie's life,


01:14:48.351 --> 01:14:49.819
and I started analyzing my life,


01:14:49.919 --> 01:14:51.487
and I started realizing that,
you know what,


01:14:51.587 --> 01:14:54.657
our lives run on these
crazy parallels.


01:14:54.757 --> 01:14:56.425
I'm going to tell you guys something.


01:14:56.525 --> 01:14:59.161
In all my previous specials,
I'd always talk about my mom.


01:14:59.261 --> 01:15:01.230
My mom, my mom, my mom, my mom.


01:15:01.330 --> 01:15:03.666
I never really made
references about my dad,


01:15:03.766 --> 01:15:06.202
because my dad wasn't there
when I was growing up.


01:15:06.302 --> 01:15:07.703
And I made a joke one time


01:15:07.803 --> 01:15:10.039
about how my mom said that
my dad was a mariachi.


01:15:10.139 --> 01:15:11.841
Which is true,
and that he was the guy


01:15:11.941 --> 01:15:13.984
that was on the bottle of hot sauce.


01:15:15.277 --> 01:15:17.548
My dad was the Tapatío guy.


01:15:18.614 --> 01:15:20.850
So after that,


01:15:20.950 --> 01:15:23.753
people started asking
questions about my dad,


01:15:23.853 --> 01:15:25.755
and so they're like, "So what's
the story with your dad?"


01:15:25.855 --> 01:15:27.690
And it felt really awkward
for me to bring up the fact


01:15:27.790 --> 01:15:29.358
that I didn't grow up
with one, you know?


01:15:29.458 --> 01:15:31.527
And nobody wants to tell people,
"I grew up without a parent."


01:15:31.627 --> 01:15:34.297
For me, it was very personal,
and I started building up this wall,


01:15:34.397 --> 01:15:37.733
because people kept asking questions,
especially this one,


01:15:37.833 --> 01:15:41.537
"What would you say if your
dad wanted to see you now?"


01:15:42.338 --> 01:15:44.106
And for me, it's been over 30 years,


01:15:44.206 --> 01:15:45.608
and so I'd always say the same thing.


01:15:45.708 --> 01:15:47.576
I said, "Well, if my
dad wanted to see me,


01:15:47.676 --> 01:15:49.856
"I'd tell him he has
to buy a ticket."


01:15:50.980 --> 01:15:52.214
That was the go-to line.


01:15:52.314 --> 01:15:54.617
"If my dad wants to see me,
I'll tell him to buy a ticket.


01:15:54.717 --> 01:15:56.191
"I'll tell him to buy a ticket.


01:15:56.318 --> 01:15:57.920
"My dad wants to see
me 30 years later,


01:15:58.020 --> 01:16:01.230
"I'm going to have him buy a ticket."
I said this for over seven years.


01:16:01.724 --> 01:16:03.369
And guess who bought a ticket?


01:16:04.693 --> 01:16:06.964
Bay Area, it's about to get real.


01:16:08.364 --> 01:16:09.732
Oh, let me tell you this one.


01:16:09.832 --> 01:16:13.169
So I'm doing a show at
a comedy club in LA


01:16:13.269 --> 01:16:14.837
called the Comedy & Magic Club,


01:16:14.937 --> 01:16:17.306
and it's about a 250-seat room,


01:16:17.406 --> 01:16:20.076
and the manager Richard comes
up to me before I go onstage,


01:16:20.176 --> 01:16:21.477
and he goes, "Hey, listen, Gabe.


01:16:21.577 --> 01:16:24.480
"Your dad's in the back of the room.
"Can we pay for his drinks and his food?"


01:16:24.580 --> 01:16:26.615
I go, "What are you talking about?"
He goes, "Yeah, your dad's in the back.


01:16:26.715 --> 01:16:27.917
"Are we paying for
his food and drinks?"


01:16:28.017 --> 01:16:30.186
I go, "Listen, I haven't seen
my dad in over 30 years.


01:16:30.286 --> 01:16:32.088
"That's somebody trying to
get free food and drinks.


01:16:32.188 --> 01:16:33.622
"Cut him off.
Don't pay for anything."


01:16:33.722 --> 01:16:35.591
"Got it, bro." So I go up onstage.


01:16:35.691 --> 01:16:38.963
I start doing my thing.
No problem, show goes over very well.


01:16:39.762 --> 01:16:42.131
I wind up outside after the show,
and I'm doing a meet-and-greet.


01:16:42.231 --> 01:16:44.266
I'm taking pictures,
and I'm signing DVDs.


01:16:44.366 --> 01:16:46.535
My dad goes out and gets in line


01:16:46.635 --> 01:16:49.305
with the rest of the fans
to try to meet me like that.


01:16:49.405 --> 01:16:51.807
And my manager goes
to the back of the line,


01:16:51.907 --> 01:16:53.542
and then she comes up
to me, and she goes,


01:16:53.642 --> 01:16:55.611
"Listen, Gabe. Um...


01:16:55.711 --> 01:16:57.313
"The guy that's at the
back of the line,


01:16:57.413 --> 01:17:01.017
"I have a feeling that
that could be your dad."


01:17:01.117 --> 01:17:03.661
I go, "Really? You, too? Why?"


01:17:03.986 --> 01:17:09.431
"I saw his ID. Gabe,
is his name Jesus?"


01:17:10.126 --> 01:17:14.103
And I was like, "Well, that's a one-in-four
chance right there, you know?"


01:17:15.898 --> 01:17:17.400
I mean, seriously,
you can't throw a rock


01:17:17.500 --> 01:17:20.644
in Los Angeles without hitting
a Jesus. You really can't.


01:17:22.304 --> 01:17:25.408
And so I go, "That's it?"


01:17:25.508 --> 01:17:29.211
She goes, "Gabe, I'm going to be
honest with you. He has your eyes."


01:17:29.311 --> 01:17:32.014
I go, "Really? Out of all of this,


01:17:32.114 --> 01:17:35.317
"you went to the two smallest things
on my body to compare us two?


01:17:35.417 --> 01:17:37.219
"Are you kidding me?"


01:17:37.319 --> 01:17:41.490
And then she said, "Is his
birthday December 25th?"


01:17:41.590 --> 01:17:45.561
And my heart dropped, because my
dad's birthday is actually Christmas.


01:17:45.661 --> 01:17:46.929
Every year, my mom would remind me,


01:17:47.029 --> 01:17:50.366
"Merry Christmas. He's not
here again. Open your gift."


01:17:50.466 --> 01:17:52.034
And so it always stuck right here,


01:17:52.134 --> 01:17:54.537
"December 25th, December 25th,
December 25th."


01:17:54.637 --> 01:17:58.841
So it freaked me out so bad that I
started shaking. I started shaking.


01:17:58.941 --> 01:18:01.143
I was freaking out, because it was
a bunch of emotions. I was angry.


01:18:01.244 --> 01:18:04.113
I was sad. I was happy. It was just
everything all at the same time.


01:18:04.213 --> 01:18:06.415
And so people are coming up to me.
They're like, "Are you okay?"


01:18:06.515 --> 01:18:08.017
And I kept saying this over and over.


01:18:08.117 --> 01:18:10.920
I said, "My dad's here. My dad's here.
My dad's here. My dad's here."


01:18:11.020 --> 01:18:13.055
And they're like, "That's good.
Your dad's here to support you."


01:18:13.155 --> 01:18:14.690
I'm like, "I haven't seen
him in over 30 years."


01:18:14.790 --> 01:18:17.436
And they're like, "Awkward.
Yeah. Awkward."


01:18:18.661 --> 01:18:21.030
So I told my manager, "Go to the
back of the line and get him out.


01:18:21.130 --> 01:18:23.199
"This is not the time or the
place to do this, right?"


01:18:23.299 --> 01:18:25.301
So she goes back there,
and I see them talking.


01:18:25.401 --> 01:18:27.703
Then I see him walking away,
and then he stops,


01:18:27.803 --> 01:18:31.040
and he turns around, and he
looks at me, and he waves.


01:18:31.140 --> 01:18:32.944
And it freaked me out, you guys.


01:18:33.409 --> 01:18:36.879
In addition to freaking me out,
he's holding one of my DVDs,


01:18:36.979 --> 01:18:40.716
which really messed me up,
because I've been signing DVDs all night.


01:18:40.816 --> 01:18:42.393
What was I going to put on his?


01:18:43.485 --> 01:18:46.029
You know? "Hey, where you been?"


01:18:51.060 --> 01:18:53.395
So she gets his info, gives it to me.


01:18:53.495 --> 01:18:56.332
I give the info to my girlfriend,
right, and I finish the meet-and-greet.


01:18:56.432 --> 01:18:58.000
We get in the car. We go back home.


01:18:58.100 --> 01:18:59.902
I'm not saying anything.
I'm just shaking.


01:19:00.002 --> 01:19:01.837
I'm sweating, and my
girl goes, "You okay?"


01:19:01.937 --> 01:19:02.938
I said, "Listen, you know what?


01:19:03.038 --> 01:19:05.908
"I been playing this scenario
in my head for over 30 years,


01:19:06.008 --> 01:19:07.409
"what would I do if
he ever showed up.


01:19:07.509 --> 01:19:09.078
"I thought I was
prepared to handle this,


01:19:09.178 --> 01:19:10.913
"and I'm not. I'm freaking out."


01:19:11.013 --> 01:19:13.249
And she goes, "Baby, listen,
how 'bout we sleep on it,


01:19:13.349 --> 01:19:14.950
"and we'll figure this
out together tomorrow?


01:19:15.050 --> 01:19:17.820
"Just sleep on it. Try to relax."
And I'm like, "That's a good idea."


01:19:17.920 --> 01:19:19.321
So we get in bed.


01:19:19.421 --> 01:19:21.857
She knocks out, and I'm just...
I can't. I can't sleep.


01:19:21.957 --> 01:19:25.361
And 3:00 in the morning rolls around,
and I say, "I can't take it anymore."


01:19:25.461 --> 01:19:27.129
So I wake up my girl.
I said, "Baby, get up."


01:19:27.229 --> 01:19:30.103
"What is it? What?"
I go, "Listen, call him."


01:19:30.366 --> 01:19:32.601
"Right now?" I go, "Yes."


01:19:32.701 --> 01:19:36.610
"It's 3:00 in the morning.
That's disrespectful."


01:19:37.406 --> 01:19:40.348
I'm like, "Hello.
Who waited 30 years?"


01:19:41.243 --> 01:19:44.980
"Fine." So she gets on the
phone, and she calls my dad,


01:19:45.080 --> 01:19:46.849
and he answered on the first ring,


01:19:46.949 --> 01:19:49.518
and I could hear 'em talking,
going back and forth.


01:19:49.618 --> 01:19:51.053
And then she hangs up
the phone, and she says,


01:19:51.153 --> 01:19:54.023
"Listen, baby, your dad
wants me to let you know


01:19:54.123 --> 01:19:56.859
"that he doesn't want any money.
He doesn't want any money.


01:19:56.959 --> 01:20:00.296
"He doesn't want anything from you,
but 10 minutes of your time,


01:20:00.396 --> 01:20:02.231
"so he can ask you a
couple of questions


01:20:02.331 --> 01:20:04.466
"and hopefully answer some questions.


01:20:04.566 --> 01:20:08.976
"Me, personally, I think you need closure,
because you're messed up."


01:20:11.907 --> 01:20:14.018
My girl keeps it real.


01:20:14.610 --> 01:20:17.290
So I said, "Listen, call him back.


01:20:17.579 --> 01:20:18.881
"Let him know that I
want to see him today


01:20:18.981 --> 01:20:20.182
"at the comedy club where we met."


01:20:20.282 --> 01:20:22.184
She goes, "Fine."
So she calls him back.


01:20:22.284 --> 01:20:25.487
She hangs up the phone. And she goes,
"Okay, baby, I confirmed it.


01:20:25.587 --> 01:20:28.691
"7:00 at the comedy club.
Can we get some sleep now?"


01:20:28.791 --> 01:20:30.659
And I go, "Listen,
I got to do something."


01:20:30.759 --> 01:20:34.863
She goes, "What?" And I get out of bed,
and I walk over to the living room.


01:20:34.963 --> 01:20:37.666
And in the living room,
I have a giant mirror, okay?


01:20:37.766 --> 01:20:39.835
And this giant mirror,
I get in front of it,


01:20:39.935 --> 01:20:43.105
and I start rehearsing the speech
I'm about to have with my dad


01:20:43.205 --> 01:20:46.508
that I haven't seen in over 30 years.
Now keep in mind, you guys.


01:20:46.608 --> 01:20:51.347
This is the only thing I've ever really
rehearsed. I can't rehearse this,


01:20:51.447 --> 01:20:55.017
because I don't know if it's funny
or not until you laugh at it.


01:20:55.117 --> 01:20:56.852
Seriously. I can stand in
front of the mirror all day.


01:21:01.056 --> 01:21:03.592
Apparently that shit is funny.
Okay, cool.


01:21:03.692 --> 01:21:05.872
I'll try it again tomorrow.


01:21:06.962 --> 01:21:08.530
So I'm front of the mirror.


01:21:08.630 --> 01:21:11.867
I'm very emotional, and my
whole chest is bright red,


01:21:11.967 --> 01:21:14.536
because I'm doing the stupid,
emotional macho thing.


01:21:14.636 --> 01:21:17.039
I'm standing there,
and I'm doing this...


01:21:17.139 --> 01:21:19.376
"Who do you think you are?


01:21:20.676 --> 01:21:22.144
"Who do you think you are?"


01:21:22.244 --> 01:21:23.812
And my girlfriend grabs me.
She grabs me.


01:21:23.912 --> 01:21:26.115
"Baby, calm down,"
and I snapped on her.


01:21:26.215 --> 01:21:29.318
I said, "Listen, this is the only way
I know how to handle this, okay?


01:21:29.418 --> 01:21:30.552
"Just give me a little bit of space.


01:21:30.652 --> 01:21:32.288
"I promise you tomorrow
I'll be a better person.


01:21:32.388 --> 01:21:34.657
"This is the only way I
know how to deal with this.


01:21:34.757 --> 01:21:38.093
"Please understand." And she
was like, "I get it, No worries.


01:21:38.193 --> 01:21:41.797
"I'm in the room if you need me."
And so she goes in the room,


01:21:41.897 --> 01:21:44.500
and I keep freaking yelling
and emotional, screaming.


01:21:44.600 --> 01:21:47.336
And later on, she told me that I
guess I was being really loud


01:21:47.436 --> 01:21:50.172
with the screaming and the hitting,
and I woke up Frankie.


01:21:50.272 --> 01:21:51.940
And she says that Frankie
came in the living room,


01:21:52.040 --> 01:21:56.145
and he hears freaking yelling and
hits and pops, and so he got scared,


01:21:56.245 --> 01:21:58.914
and he sees me standing there in front
of the mirror, and I'm hitting myself,


01:21:59.014 --> 01:22:02.285
and he freaked out. He was like...
"I better take out the trash."


01:22:10.392 --> 01:22:13.028
Now don't get me wrong.
It's hysterical now, but at that moment,


01:22:13.128 --> 01:22:14.430
I didn't notice it,
and I didn't care.


01:22:14.530 --> 01:22:16.598
My focus is, "What am I
gonna say to this man?"


01:22:16.698 --> 01:22:21.637
So I'm freaking nailing myself,
and finally, okay, 6:45 rolls around.


01:22:21.737 --> 01:22:24.873
My girl and I get in the car,
and we head over to the comedy club.


01:22:24.973 --> 01:22:26.942
We get there about 15 minutes late,
and you know what?


01:22:27.042 --> 01:22:28.711
I'm like, "That's cool.
I'm glad we're late.


01:22:28.811 --> 01:22:30.612
"This way, I'll just
walk into the club,


01:22:30.712 --> 01:22:32.348
"I'll look at him,
and I'll start talking."


01:22:32.448 --> 01:22:34.183
We pull up to the front.


01:22:34.883 --> 01:22:38.687
I get out of the car. I start
walking up towards the sidewalk.


01:22:38.787 --> 01:22:43.127
My dad is also 15 minutes late,
and he's walking up the curb,


01:22:44.660 --> 01:22:45.828
It caught me off-guard,


01:22:45.928 --> 01:22:47.896
because I wasn't planning on
meeting him on the street.


01:22:47.996 --> 01:22:50.739
I was gonna meet him inside.
So now I froze. I'm like...


01:22:52.267 --> 01:22:55.607
He looks at me,
and he does this. He goes...


01:23:00.409 --> 01:23:01.977
And for me, I'm like,
"You know what? No.


01:23:02.077 --> 01:23:04.913
"You can't do this. You can't show up
after 30 years and expect to hug me."


01:23:05.013 --> 01:23:08.484
In my head, I'm like, "Do I
hug him, or do I hit him?"


01:23:08.584 --> 01:23:11.053
And if my girl wouldn't have been
there, I'd have laid his ass out.


01:23:11.153 --> 01:23:14.323
You don't do that to a kid.
And seriously, you guys. You can't.


01:23:14.423 --> 01:23:16.425
You can't just show up...


01:23:23.432 --> 01:23:26.502
And so my girl's looking at me, and she
sees the situation from different eyes,


01:23:26.602 --> 01:23:29.338
so she's like, "You know what?
Just..." And I'm like...


01:23:29.438 --> 01:23:32.274
So I walk over to him,
and I put my arms around him,


01:23:32.374 --> 01:23:33.375
and I'll be honest with you guys.


01:23:33.475 --> 01:23:36.612
It was the emptiest hug
I have ever given anyone,


01:23:36.712 --> 01:23:38.914
and I'm hugging him,
and he starts crying in my ear.


01:23:39.014 --> 01:23:41.116
And when he starts crying in
my ear, I pushed him off.


01:23:41.216 --> 01:23:43.352
And I go, "No,
don't even try that. No."


01:23:43.452 --> 01:23:45.721
And he's like, "No, no, no.
No, no. Sorry. Sorry. Sorry."


01:23:45.821 --> 01:23:48.457
And I go, "Turn around.
Go inside." And he's, "Okay, okay."


01:23:48.557 --> 01:23:50.592
So he turns around, and he goes
inside, and I start shaking.


01:23:50.692 --> 01:23:52.861
And my girl's like, "You okay?"
I go, "I'm freaking out right now."


01:23:52.961 --> 01:23:54.363
She goes, "Baby, just breathe."
I'm like...


01:23:54.463 --> 01:23:57.099
I'm trying to breathe and relax,
and he walks in.


01:23:57.199 --> 01:24:00.102
Five minutes go by, and I go,
"All right. Here we go."


01:24:00.202 --> 01:24:01.804
I walk inside.


01:24:01.904 --> 01:24:05.574
I see him sitting down, and I
walk right up to him, and I go...


01:24:05.974 --> 01:24:08.110
"Who do you think you are?"


01:24:08.210 --> 01:24:10.479
And my girlfriend grabbed me.
"Baby, calm down."


01:24:10.579 --> 01:24:13.453
"Hey, you saw me doing
the shit at the house."


01:24:18.520 --> 01:24:21.089
And I started laying into my dad.
I started asking all the questions.


01:24:21.189 --> 01:24:23.892
You know, "Why? Where were you?
What happened? Did you even try?


01:24:23.992 --> 01:24:26.395
"What was going on in your
life that was so important?"


01:24:26.495 --> 01:24:28.230
And what really bothered
me was the fact


01:24:28.330 --> 01:24:32.234
that he had a solid answer for every
single question I hit him with,


01:24:32.334 --> 01:24:34.837
because I compared 'em to notes
from my mom and my sisters.


01:24:34.937 --> 01:24:36.472
And more than anything,
I just wanted to vent.


01:24:36.572 --> 01:24:39.007
I wanted to yell. I wanted to cuss.
I wanted to scream.


01:24:39.107 --> 01:24:41.944
I wanted to get physical, and he
wasn't giving me that opportunity.


01:24:42.044 --> 01:24:45.315
So I started making stuff up.
I was like, "You know what, Dad?


01:24:46.848 --> 01:24:49.221
"My mom was mean to
me because of you."


01:24:50.919 --> 01:24:52.788
He goes, "Well,
you know something, mijo,


01:24:52.888 --> 01:24:57.092
"your mother had to do whatever
she had to do to raise you right.


01:24:57.192 --> 01:24:59.528
"Look how good you turned out."


01:24:59.628 --> 01:25:02.001
I was like, "Oh, he's good."


01:25:03.932 --> 01:25:06.476
Apparently he has a mirror, too.


01:25:09.471 --> 01:25:10.939
So I kept hitting him
with more questions,


01:25:11.039 --> 01:25:13.108
and he kept coming back
with solid answers.


01:25:13.208 --> 01:25:14.376
Eventually I got to the point


01:25:14.476 --> 01:25:17.546
where I started asking the same
questions over and over again,


01:25:17.646 --> 01:25:19.615
to see if maybe I'd get
a different answer,


01:25:19.715 --> 01:25:22.784
and it started becoming redundant,
and I'm just going in circles.


01:25:22.884 --> 01:25:26.455
Next thing I know,
my dad starts cracking jokes.


01:25:26.555 --> 01:25:29.825
I'm like, "Seriously? 30 years,
and now you're cracking jokes?"


01:25:29.925 --> 01:25:33.529
And it's not so much that he was
cracking jokes that bothered me.


01:25:33.629 --> 01:25:36.002
It's the fact that they were funny.


01:25:37.399 --> 01:25:41.643
I mean, really funny.
My girl's laughing.


01:25:42.671 --> 01:25:45.614
Yeah, I could hear her. "Really?"


01:25:46.575 --> 01:25:50.579
"What? What? I see where you get it."


01:25:50.679 --> 01:25:53.723
I'm like, "Cabrona,
you're gonna get it."


01:25:55.283 --> 01:25:56.723
"Yeah."


01:25:57.653 --> 01:25:59.688
So the conversation is not
going anywhere. So I said,


01:25:59.788 --> 01:26:01.156
"You know what? Let me
just change the subject.


01:26:01.256 --> 01:26:03.559
"Let me just try to talk to
this guy as a regular person,


01:26:03.659 --> 01:26:05.060
"not try to meet him as my dad."


01:26:05.160 --> 01:26:07.496
So next thing I know, we got on
the subject of entertainment,


01:26:07.596 --> 01:26:08.931
and that's something that
we both clicked with,


01:26:09.031 --> 01:26:11.433
because my dad was a
mariachi for 20 years,


01:26:11.533 --> 01:26:12.834
and of course I do what I do,


01:26:12.934 --> 01:26:15.337
but the stories and the
lifestyle is very similar.


01:26:15.437 --> 01:26:17.473
You know, we shared a lot of stories
about being at train stations,


01:26:17.574 --> 01:26:19.408
bus stations, the airports,
being in the shuttle vans,


01:26:19.508 --> 01:26:21.843
being in this city, that city,
this country, that country,


01:26:21.943 --> 01:26:23.412
coming home to a family
that missed you,


01:26:23.512 --> 01:26:27.015
or coming home to a family that
only wanted to see the check.


01:26:27.115 --> 01:26:30.659
Next thing I know, we started
laughing. He high-fived me.


01:26:30.786 --> 01:26:33.689
And then I told him, I says, "You know
what? I got to be honest with you."


01:26:33.789 --> 01:26:36.525
I said, "I tried. I tried to
hate you. I really did."


01:26:36.625 --> 01:26:40.228
I go, "But I understand.
I understand what happened.


01:26:40.328 --> 01:26:43.265
"And I just want to let you know that
I'm very grateful that you're here,


01:26:43.365 --> 01:26:47.202
"and I'm sorry that I was
the way that I was, um..."


01:26:48.737 --> 01:26:50.041
Yeah.


01:26:57.813 --> 01:26:59.448
I says, "I think I
really needed this."


01:26:59.548 --> 01:27:01.483
And he goes, "I know I
needed this." I go, "Yeah."


01:27:01.583 --> 01:27:03.785
I go, "Listen, I want to
ask you for a favor."


01:27:03.885 --> 01:27:06.555
"Anything. Tu dime.
Whatever. Tu dime."


01:27:06.655 --> 01:27:10.058
I go, "Look, I don't have
any pictures of you,


01:27:10.158 --> 01:27:11.860
"of my mom, and myself together.


01:27:11.993 --> 01:27:13.895
"It would mean a lot to me
if I could get a picture


01:27:13.995 --> 01:27:16.398
"with the three of us together,
so that I could blow it up and frame it


01:27:16.498 --> 01:27:18.400
"and put it on my wall.
Would you be willing


01:27:18.500 --> 01:27:20.068
"to take a picture with
my mom and myself?"


01:27:20.168 --> 01:27:24.406
"No problem. You name it.
Yo lo hago. No problem."


01:27:24.506 --> 01:27:26.008
I go, "Okay, thank you.
I appreciate that."


01:27:26.108 --> 01:27:28.247
I go, "I just gotta check
with my mom." And he goes...


01:27:31.413 --> 01:27:33.218
And I was like, "Right?"


01:27:38.086 --> 01:27:39.488
So I gave him a real hug.


01:27:39.588 --> 01:27:43.091
I got his direct info, and, you know,
he told me he was going back to Mexico


01:27:43.191 --> 01:27:45.260
for eight months and he
was gonna come back,


01:27:45.360 --> 01:27:46.628
and he told me I had two sisters


01:27:46.728 --> 01:27:49.331
that I didn't know about,
and I couldn't take it.


01:27:49.431 --> 01:27:51.800
My girl and I, we get in the car,
and we go to my mom's house.


01:27:53.468 --> 01:27:55.504
We get to the front,
and my mom hears the car,


01:27:55.604 --> 01:27:58.206
so she comes outside, and she's like...


01:27:58.306 --> 01:27:59.374
"What are you doing here?"


01:27:59.474 --> 01:28:03.417
I go, "Mom, you are not gonna
believe who I just had dinner with."


01:28:03.779 --> 01:28:05.287
"Obama."


01:28:09.751 --> 01:28:11.286
I go, "No, Ma, this is actually


01:28:11.386 --> 01:28:12.788
"bigger than Obama,
if you could believe this."


01:28:12.888 --> 01:28:14.056
I say, "Let's go inside the house.


01:28:14.156 --> 01:28:15.957
"This is a good one,
a really good one."


01:28:18.493 --> 01:28:21.396
So she goes inside. I follow her,
my girl's behind me.


01:28:21.496 --> 01:28:24.700
Close the door. I go, "Mom, listen."
I says, "I need you to sit down,


01:28:24.800 --> 01:28:26.401
"because what I got to
tell you is a big deal."


01:28:26.501 --> 01:28:28.970
"Mijo, don't tell me to sit
down in my own house."


01:28:31.740 --> 01:28:33.375
I go, "Mom, trust me.
You want to sit down."


01:28:34.843 --> 01:28:37.614
"What? Who did you have
dinner with? Who? Who?"


01:28:39.014 --> 01:28:41.917
"I had dinner with my dad."
And she got all Telemundo.


01:28:45.821 --> 01:28:48.323
And I hugged her, and she's
being like an octopus.


01:28:51.626 --> 01:28:53.328
And I'm trying to grab her,
and I'm looking at my girl.


01:28:53.428 --> 01:28:57.374
"Baby, help me." She's like,
"That's your mom. This is you."


01:28:58.767 --> 01:29:01.269
I finally get my mom to
sit down, and she's...


01:29:02.537 --> 01:29:04.506
And she keeps saying the same thing
over and over. "El dinero. El dinero."


01:29:04.606 --> 01:29:06.842
"The money. The money.
He wants the money.


01:29:06.942 --> 01:29:08.644
"El cabron has cable now."


01:29:10.879 --> 01:29:14.527
When she finally calmed down,
the first thing she says to me is...


01:29:15.784 --> 01:29:17.691
"Who looks better?"


01:29:18.386 --> 01:29:20.856
"Are you serious, Mom?
You're 11 years older than him,


01:29:20.956 --> 01:29:22.224
"and you want to know
who looks better?"


01:29:22.324 --> 01:29:23.692
"I'm just asking."


01:29:23.792 --> 01:29:27.499
"You look better." "You're such a liar."
"Why are you asking me?"


01:29:29.231 --> 01:29:31.099
"What does he want?


01:29:31.199 --> 01:29:32.434
"He wanted to buy a car?
What? What, what, what?"


01:29:32.534 --> 01:29:34.236
I go, "Mom, listen.


01:29:34.536 --> 01:29:36.438
"My dad, all he wanted


01:29:36.538 --> 01:29:38.373
"was he just wanted to talk to me.
He doesn't want any money.


01:29:38.473 --> 01:29:39.541
"He doesn't want me
to buy him anything.


01:29:39.641 --> 01:29:41.810
"He's got a ranch in Mexico.
He's very happy.


01:29:41.910 --> 01:29:43.945
"He just wanted to talk
and ask some questions


01:29:44.045 --> 01:29:47.048
"and hopefully answer some
questions, and he told me...


01:29:47.148 --> 01:29:48.984
"Mom, Mom, my dad told me


01:29:49.084 --> 01:29:51.720
"that I have two sisters
that I didn't know about."


01:29:51.820 --> 01:29:54.523
"Ah. I could have
told you about that."


01:30:00.595 --> 01:30:03.064
"You knew I had two sisters,
and you didn't tell me?"


01:30:03.164 --> 01:30:05.901
"Mijo, you don't like
the ones you have now.


01:30:07.402 --> 01:30:08.970
"I'm going to burden you
con otras dos cabronas


01:30:09.070 --> 01:30:12.107
"que no les va a hablar,
ni a mandar e-mail?"


01:30:16.678 --> 01:30:19.748
So we talked for a good hour,
and then I brought it up.


01:30:19.848 --> 01:30:22.484
I said, "Listen, Mom,
I asked my dad for a favor."


01:30:22.584 --> 01:30:24.753
"You should. You should ask
him for many favors..."


01:30:34.562 --> 01:30:36.531
I go, "Look, Mom, I'm going to
ask you for the same favor."


01:30:37.933 --> 01:30:39.768
"Why? Why am I getting...


01:30:39.868 --> 01:30:42.103
"I raised you while this cabron
was at the bars in Tijuana...


01:30:44.973 --> 01:30:46.174
<i>"Why are..."</i>


01:30:46.942 --> 01:30:51.680
I go, "Mom, listen. It's an easy favor,
but it's a hard one, okay?


01:30:51.780 --> 01:30:56.818
"I don't have any pictures of you,
my dad, and me together."


01:30:56.918 --> 01:30:59.587
"Because he wasn't there!" "Mom!


01:30:59.955 --> 01:31:01.323
"I know he wasn't there,
but you know what?


01:31:01.423 --> 01:31:05.493
"He's here now, and he's not that bad.
It would mean a lot to me if I could


01:31:05.593 --> 01:31:07.796
"take a picture with the
two of you and myself,


01:31:07.896 --> 01:31:10.031
"so that I could frame it
and put it on my wall."


01:31:10.131 --> 01:31:11.533
"Why?"


01:31:11.633 --> 01:31:13.001
"Mom, so that when people
come over to the house,


01:31:13.101 --> 01:31:15.303
"I can point and go, 'Look.
Those are my parents.'


01:31:15.403 --> 01:31:16.638
"I've never been able to do that.


01:31:16.738 --> 01:31:20.613
"It would mean a lot to be able to say,
'Look, these are my parents.'


01:31:20.842 --> 01:31:22.612
"One picture.


01:31:24.079 --> 01:31:25.952
"Just one picture."


01:31:27.582 --> 01:31:29.584
So I says, "Would you
please entertain the idea


01:31:29.684 --> 01:31:31.653
"of getting together with
us for a few minutes,


01:31:31.753 --> 01:31:33.188
"so that we could just
take some pictures?"


01:31:33.288 --> 01:31:35.223
And she goes, "No, mijo,
you can't ask me to do that."


01:31:35.323 --> 01:31:38.660
And we went back and forth for a while,
and she eventually agreed to it.


01:31:38.760 --> 01:31:40.829
She goes, "Okay, pero not right now.


01:31:40.929 --> 01:31:44.366
"Ahorita no, mijo. No, I have to get
ready." I'm like, "Oh, God, really?"


01:31:44.466 --> 01:31:47.068
I go, "Well, Mom, here's the deal.
My dad went back to Mexico.


01:31:47.168 --> 01:31:50.405
"He'll be back here in eight months.
Is that long enough?"


01:31:50.505 --> 01:31:52.651
"I can do eight months.


01:31:52.841 --> 01:31:55.647
"Eight months, sí, se puede.
Eight months, okay."


01:31:55.810 --> 01:31:57.879
"All right." And I was
excited, you guys.


01:31:57.979 --> 01:32:00.348
We had a date planned out,
we had a restaurant, and I told her,


01:32:00.448 --> 01:32:02.050
"Look, here's how
it's gonna work, Mom.


01:32:02.150 --> 01:32:03.184
"We're gonna walk
into this restaurant.


01:32:03.284 --> 01:32:05.220
"You're gonna sit on my left,
he's gonna sit on my right,


01:32:05.320 --> 01:32:07.355
"and we're gonna take three pictures,
and then we're gonna leave,


01:32:07.455 --> 01:32:10.359
"and I'm going to take
you to Golden Corral."


01:32:12.293 --> 01:32:15.337
And she tells me, "Why does
he get to sit on the right?"


01:32:15.563 --> 01:32:18.073
"You can sit on the right.
Oh, my God!"


01:32:19.567 --> 01:32:23.271
So the date was set eight months later,
and unfortunately for me,


01:32:23.371 --> 01:32:26.245
- my mom died before I got that opportunity.
- Aw!


01:32:27.008 --> 01:32:28.777
The only bright side to the picture,


01:32:28.877 --> 01:32:32.714
is that I got a photo
from my brother...


01:32:32.814 --> 01:32:38.053
It's a black-and-white picture, 8x10.
It's a picture of my mom and my dad


01:32:38.153 --> 01:32:41.790
